TI  - STUDIES ON MINERAL AND PROTEIN UTILIZATION BY ATLANTIC SALMON (SALMO SALAR) GROWN IN SEA WATER
AU  - LALL, S.P.
AU  - BISHOP, F.J.
AU  - LALL, S.P.
AU  - BISHOP, F.J.
PY  - 1977
AB  - THE PRESENT REPORT SUMMARIZES THE RESULTS OF FOUR EXPERIMENTS DESIGNED TO STUDY MINERAL AND PROTEIN UTILIZATION BY ATLANTIC SALMON. (SALMO SALAR) GROWN IN SEAWATER. THESE NUTRIENTS ARE DISCUSSED SEPARATELY IN TWO SECTIONS. THE FIRST SECTION DEALS WITH THE UTILIZATION OF CALCIUM, PHOSPHOROUS, MAGNESIUM, SODIUM, POTASSIUM, COBALT, COPPER, MANGANESE, ZINC, IRON, IODINE AND FLUORINE FROM SEAWATER AND FEED. THE RESPONSE WAS MEASURED IN TERMS OF GROWTH, FEED UTILIZATION, BONE ASH CONTENT, HEMATOCRIT, HEMOGLOBIN AND MINERAL LEVEL IN BLOOD. RESULTS INDICATE THAT FISH CAN DERIVE CALCIUM FROM SEAWATER PROVIDED SUFFICIENT PHOSPHOROUS IS PRESENT IN THE DIET. THE ADDITION OF SODIUM, POTASSIUM AND MAGNESIUM WAS ALSO NOT NECESSARY IN THE DIET. HOWEVER, PHOSPHOROUS AND TRACE ELEMENT (ZINC, COPPER, COBALT, IRON, MANGANESE, IODINE AND FLUORINE) SUPPLEMENTATION WAS REQUIRED FOR OPTIMUM GROWTH, FOOD UTILIIZATION, BONE MINERALIZATION AND NORMAL HEMOGLOBIN, HEMATOCRIT AND MINERAL LEVELS IN THE BLOOD. THE SECOND SECTION CONSISTED OF EXPERIMENTS ON PROTEIN REQUIREMENTS, EFFECTS OF VARYING CALORIE AND PROTEIN RATIOS IN THE DIET AND THE DIGESTIBILITY OF VARIOUS PROTEINS IN SEAWATER. BASED UPON GROWTH, FEED EFFICIENCY, MORTALITY, BODY COMPOSITION AND LIVER COMPOSITION DATA, THE PROTEIN REQUIREMENT OF ATLANTIC SALMON WAS ESTIMATED TO BE APPROXIMATELY 45 G PROTEIN PER 100 G OF DRY DIET. GENERALLY, THE INCREASE IN DIETARY FAT AND PROTEIN LEVEL IMPROVED THE GROWTH AND FEED EFFICIENCY. DIET CONTAINING LOW PROTEIN, HIGH CARBOHYDRATE AND HIGH FAT RESULTED IN INCREASED LIVER GLYCOGEN DEPOSITION. A DIETARY BALANCE OF CARBOHYDRATE, LIPID AND PROTEIN WAS ESSENTIAL TO OVERCOME THE HIGH LIVER GLYCOGEN ACCUMULATION. GROWTH AND BIOCHEMICAL DATA SUGGEST THAT DIET CONTAINING 45 PERCENT PROTEIN AND 20 PERCENT FAT SHOWED THE OPTIMUM RESPONSE. THE DIGESTIBILITY OF CASEIN, FISH PROTEIN CONCENTRATE (FPC), SOYBEAN PROTEIN AND FISH MEAL WAS ESTIMATED EMPLOYING AND INDIRECT METHOD USING CHROMIC OXIDE AS DIGESTION INDICATOR. CASEIN (94.1 PERCENT) WAS DIGESTED SIGNIFICANTLY BETTER THAN FPC (91.5 PERCENT), SOYBEAN PROTEIN (86.4 PERCENT) AND FISH MEAL (77.7 PERCENT). INCREASING THE LEVEL OF THESE PROTEINS IN THE DIET RESULTED IN A DECREASE IN PROTEIN DIGESTIBILITY. THE REDUCTION IN PROTEIN DIGESTIBILITY WAS MORE PRONOUNCED WITH FISH MEAL AND SOYBEAN THAN FPC AND CASEIN. TAB, 28 REF.
